# Produced by the published code: bench_cache_residency.py --anonymise
# Session ids are sha256 prefixes emitted by --anonymise. Token counts are untouched.
ARM 1 -- RESIDENT: one process, two turns over stream-json stdin
  resident turn1: cache_read=26219 cache_creation=0 input=2863
  resident turn2: cache_read=0 cache_creation=54692 input=1054

ARM 2 -- FRESH: turn 1 in one process, turn 2 in a NEW process via --resume
  fresh turn1: {'sid': 'sid-4a97569a', 'read': 26219, 'create': 0, 'inp': 2863}
  fresh turn2 (new process, resumed, inside TTL): {'sid': 'sid-4a97569a', 'read': 29094, 'create': 0, 'inp': 2}

ARM 3 -- SUFFIX PERTURBATION (kept, and it FAILED as a control)
  perturbed turn2: {'sid': 'sid-4a97569a', 'read': 14848, 'create': 14304, 'inp': 2}

ARM 4 -- PREFIX PERTURBATION, the real control: switch model to 'haiku' (caches are keyed per model)
  model-switched turn2: {'sid': 'sid-4a97569a', 'read': 21132, 'create': 9742, 'inp': 10}

ARM 5 -- DEAD-WRITER TEST: does a cache written by an EXITED process get read by a new one?
  A (writer; exits before B and C run): {'sid': 'sid-55671f08', 'read': 14848, 'create': 11416, 'inp': 2863}
  B (new process, SAME marker as the dead writer):      {'sid': 'sid-55671f08', 'read': 26264, 'create': 2875, 'inp': 2}
  C (new process, DIFFERENT marker -- the control):     {'sid': 'sid-55671f08', 'read': 14848, 'create': 14307, 'inp': 164}

READING
  resident 2nd-turn cache_read=0   fresh 2nd-turn cache_read=29094
  NOT like-for-like: the resident arm's turn 2 follows a longer cached prefix. Do not quote this pair as residency being cheaper.
  suffix-perturbed cache_read=14848 vs unperturbed 29094  <- did NOT drop; either way this arm is NOT a reliable control (--append-system-prompt is a SUFFIX change)
  model-switched hit_frac=68% (read=21132 creation=9742) vs unperturbed hit_frac=100% (read=29094 creation=0)
    PRIMARY (hit-fraction) -> control HOLDS (the instrument can show a miss)
    SUPERSEDED (read-ratio) -> control FAILED  [kept visible: this is the test that misread a 4.9x creation increase as no-miss]

VERDICT
  Arm 2: a FRESH process resuming the session read 29094 cached tokens (creation=0, the new suffix). Residency was not required for a cache hit.
  But on a busy box arm 2's prefix may ALREADY have been warm from an earlier run, in which case it shows a hit without showing WHOSE write was read. Arm 5 is the arm that separates those.
  Arm 5: B (same marker as the dead writer) read 26264; C (different marker) read 14848.
  => B read MORE than C by 11416 tokens. The only difference between them is whether the marker matches what the now-EXITED process A wrote. A cache entry outlived the process that created it and was read by a different one.
     EXACT: B_read(26264) == C_read(14848) + A_creation(11416). B read precisely the bytes A wrote, on top of the prefix both share. Token-exact, not directional.
  LIMIT: n=1 per arm throughout. Supported, not settled.
